/*!
\page qtbridges-csharp-index.html
\title Qt Bridges - C#
\brief Bring QML/Qt Quick to C#/.NET with a single package.

This bundle ships:

\list
    \li \b {.NET adapter} to host the Qt/QML engine from managed code.
    \li \b {Source/code generator} that discovers your C# types and exposes
        them to QML.
    \li \b {Filtering rules} (\c{[Qt.Include]}, \c{[Qt.Ignore]},
        \c{[Qt.IgnoreType]}) to control what becomes visible to QML.
    \li \b {C++ include headers} used by the native side of the bridge.
    \li A \b {minimal, open-source Qt Quick runtime subset} sufficient to
        load and run QML (no full or commercial Qt installation).
\endlist

\section1 Getting started

To get started, see the \l{Getting Started with Qt Quick C#} tutorial.

\section1 How it works


\list 1
    \li Reference the package in your .NET 8 project.
    \li Mark types/members you want to expose to QML using attributes:
        \list
            \li \c {[Qt.Include]} - opt-in a type or a specific member.
            \li \c {[Qt.Ignore]} - remove a type or member.
            \li \c {[assembly: Qt.IgnoreType(...)]} - remove one or more types,
                or entire type hierarchies by rule; supports \c{Inherited = true}.
        \endlist
    \li Add your QML (e.g., \c{Main.qml}) to the project.
    \li Build & run. The generator creates the interop surface; the adapter boots
        the QML engine and loads your entry file.
\endlist


\section2 Add Qt Quick C# to your project

To start using the Qt Quick C# in your project, add the \c Qt.Bridge.CSharp
package to your project:

\badcode
# Run in the project folder
dotnet add package QtGroup.Qt.Bridge.CSharp.win-x64 --version 0.1.0.1-alpha
\endcode

\section2 Select the types of your data Model to expose to QML Views

By default, all public types in the project are exposed to QML.

\code
// This class will be included
public class Foo
{
    // This property will be included
    public int Bar { get; set; }
}
\endcode

To control what gets exposed or hidden, mark types/members with attributes:

\list
\li \c {[Qt.Include]} - expose a type or a specific member.
\li \c {[Qt.Ignore]} - remove a type or member.
\li \c {[assembly: Qt.IgnoreType(...)]} - remove one or more types, or entire type
   hierarchies by rule; supports \c{Inherited = true}.
\endlist

For example:

\code
[assembly: Qt.IgnoreType(typeof(Foo), Inherited = true)]

// This class will be ignored (ignored base type)
public class Foo
{ }

// This class will be ignored (derived type of ignored base type)
public class Oof : Foo
{ }

// This class will be included (opted-in by attribute)
[Qt.Include]
public class Bar : Foo
{ }
\endcode

\section2 Add a QML View to your project

Create a QML file (e.g., \c{Main.qml}) that uses your C# types.

For example, given the following C# type:

\code
using Qt.Bridge.Utils;

namespace MyProject
{
    public class MyClass
    {
        public int MyProperty { get; set; }
    }
}
\endcode

To access C# types from QML, instantiate them in QML and access their members
via a \c {.} operator. The member names match the C# names, but are started in
lowercase:

\code
import QtQuick
// ...
Rectangle {
    width: 400; height: 400

    MyClass {
        id: myClassInstance
    }

    Text {
        text: "MyProperty value is " + myClassInstance.myProperty
        anchors.centerIn: parent
    }
}
\endcode

\section2 Load the QML View from C# code

In your C# code, load the QML file using \c{Qml.LoadFromRootModule}:

\code
using Qt.Quick;

namespace MyProject
{
    internal class Program
    {
        static void Main(string[] args)
        {
            Qml.LoadFromRootModule("Main");
            Qml.WaitForExit();
        }
    }
}
\endcode

Build and run. The generator creates the interop surface; the adapter boots
the QML engine and loads your entry file.

\section1 Tutorial

This tutorial shows how to create, run, and modify a Qt Quick C# application:
\l{Getting Started with Qt Quick C#}{Getting Started with Qt Quick C#}.

\section1 How-to guides

\section2 Using project and item templates

See \l{Using project and item templates} for instructions on using the provided
project and item templates to create Qt Quick C# applications.

\section2 Selecting types to expose to QML Views

See \l{Using C\\# Models with QML Views} for instructions on selecting
the types to expose to QML Views.

\section2 Debugging generated code

See \l{debugging generated code} for instructions on debugging
the generated native code.

\section2 Building from source

See \l{Building from source} for instructions on building the Qt Bridges
package from source.

*/
